---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="About">
  <article class="about">
    <header class="about-header">
      <p class="meta">About</p>
      <h1>The Story So Far</h1>
    </header>

    <div class="prose drop-cap">
      <p>
        I started out as a civil engineer. Graduated, got a job as a structural engineer, 
        and spent a year doing the work. Then COVID hit and everything changed. Our firm 
        was strictly office-based. All the tools and software licenses lived on company machines. 
        When lockdown came, only senior staff were allowed in. Juniors like me were told to 
        stay home. No remote setup. No work to do. Just time.
        
        I spent that time on Twitter (now X). That's where I found Product School.
        I took the course, started reading everything I could find, and fell into 
        a growing community of people either learning product management or already doing it.
        Something clicked. The way I thought about problems, systems, trade-offs. It felt 
        closer to how my brain actually worked than structural calculations ever did.
        
        While all this was happening, I was back home in Nigeria. We had no return date from 
        the firm, so I went back. That's where I met a founder running a venture studio. He 
        was looking for someone to lead the build of a new product. These tools were 
        exploding during that period and he needed someone who could move fast. I pitched myself. 
        I had a short product course, a lot of self-directed learning, and something he 
        didn't expect: an engineering background. While I was in university studying civil engineering, 
        I'd also been teaching myself software development. I built a startup with friends during 
        that time. We raised money for it and pushed it as far as we could.

        He gave me three months to prove I could do the job. That was all I needed. I embedded 
        myself with the engineering team from day one. I understood how they thought, what 
        slowed them down, and where product decisions were creating unnecessary friction. 
        The probation turned into a role. The role turned into a career.
      </p>

      <h2>What I've built.</h2>

      <p>
        Since then I've worked across fintech, payments, and HRTech. At Hands In, I joined as 
        the first product hire and built the product function from scratch. I led the 0-to-1 
        development of a split-payment checkout API, onboarded 10+ merchant businesses, and 
        designed payment flows that hit an 87.8% checkout conversion rate. I built multi-card 
        retry logic that recovered €2.4M+ in previously failed payments at a 95.1% recovery rate. 
        At Omnipresent, I took on payroll processing across 50+ countries, reduced tax document 
        distribution from multi-hour cycles to near real-time, and built monitoring infrastructure 
        that helped the team catch processing failures 3x faster.
      </p>

      <h2>What I'm doing now.</h2>

      <p>
        Right now I'm at Baylis Digital Infrastructures, working on investment strategy and digital 
        infrastructure across African markets. I lead the evaluation of data centre and Bitcoin 
        mining opportunities across a $500M+ pipeline, build financial model frameworks deployed 
        across 10+ projects, and manage cross-functional workstreams spanning legal, technical, 
        and commercial inputs. It's early-stage, high-complexity work. The kind where you're building 
        the playbook as you go.
      </p>

      <h2>Why Product Management</h2>

      <p>
        I love product management because it sits at the intersection of everything I care about: 
        systems thinking, data, cross-functional collaboration, and building things that actually 
        work for real people. I'm especially interested in how AI and automation are changing the 
        way product and operations teams work. Not as abstract concepts, but as practical tools 
        that make teams faster and decision-making sharper. I'm currently looking for my next 
        Senior PM or Product Operations role where I can bring that combination of analytical depth,
         operational rigour, and shipping speed to a team solving hard problems.
      </p>

      <h2>Outside Work</h2>

      <p>
        I'm a lifelong Real Madrid fan. One of my bucket list items is walking into the 
        Santiago Bernabeu and watching my team play live. It hasn't happened yet, but it will. 
        I'm also trying to walk one million combined steps in 2026, which at the time of 
        writing means I need to average roughly 2,740 steps a day for the rest of the year. 
        Some days I'm ahead. Some days my sofa is winning. The scoreboard is public and the 
        sofa knows it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about {
    max-width: var(--max-width);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 4rem 2rem 6rem;
  }

  .about-header {
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  .about-header .meta {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    display: block;
  }

  .about-header h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4rem);
  }
</style>
